[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I exercised the inter‑rack fabric (leafs, spines, and the super‑spine) while probing between the two PCs and found no evidence of congestion, flaps, or loss during the test window. End‑to‑end pings between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) showed 0% loss with steady RTTs around 1–5 ms over 100 probes each direction. All BGP adjacencies across the fabric are Established and stable; each router has the correct /24 routes installed, and the leaves prefer a single stable next hop (no ECMP oscillation). Queueing/TC on every interconnect (super_spine eth0/1; spines eth0/1/2; leaves uplinks) shows fq_codel roots with zero drops and zero backlog, and link counters (ip -s link) report 0 RX/TX errors or drops across the fabric. ARP/neighbor tables are normal.

Mechanism-wise, if the fabric were “jamming,” we would expect packet loss, rising queuing backlog, ECN/drops, interface errors, or BGP churn—none are present. Localization therefore points away from the spine/super‑spine interconnects and toward factors outside the L3 fabric (endpoint application, host CPU, local NIC/driver, or higher‑layer dependencies like DNS).

Root cause: Not reproduced on the network; no fabric congestion or instability observed.

Corrective fix: No network changes recommended. To catch intermittent endpoint issues, please: run a long mtr/ping from both PCs during a freeze, enable an iperf3 server on pc_0_1 and run sustained tests, and capture host CPU/dmesg/NIC stats. If a time window is known, we can deploy continuous pings/telemetry to correlate. 
=== END ===